/-!
# Finiteness of the zero locus of jmath̄ - j₀

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.exists_finset_ord_jBar_sub_pos`
(`a1bd7734-32e6-5f5f-b5d8-f9cd122118bd`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `ac7c507d-cc79-55ea-a71e-825ca6c0ac54`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $N$ be a natural number, assumed nonzero, and let $j_0$ be an element of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$
(the Mathlib algebraic closure of $\mathbb{Q}$). Write $F_N =$ `modularFunctionFieldFull N` for the
subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the divisor expansions at level $N$,
and let $\bar F_N =$ `modularFunctionFieldBar N` be its base change, namely the intermediate field
of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ generated by the image of $F_N$ under
the coefficientwise map `coeffEmb` induced by $\mathbb{Q} \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$.
Let $\bar\jmath =$ `jBar N` be the element of $\bar F_N$ obtained by applying `coeffEmb` to the
$q$-expansion `jq` of the $j$-invariant. The assertion is that there exists a finite set $S$ of
places of $\bar F_N$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ — a place being a proper valuation subring of
$\bar F_N$ containing the image of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and being a principal ideal ring — such
that, for every such place $v$, membership $v \in S$ holds if and only if $0 <
\operatorname{ord}_v(\bar\jmath - j_0)$, where $\operatorname{ord}_v$ is minus the logarithm of the
associated adic valuation and $j_0$ is viewed in $\bar F_N$ via the structure map. Thus $S$ is
exactly the set of places at which $\bar\jmath - j_0$ has strictly positive order, and in particular
that set is finite.

This is the finiteness of the zero divisor of the non-constant function $\bar\jmath - j_0$ on the
modular curve of level $N$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, packaged as a `Finset` so that sums of
local orders over the fibre above $j = j_0$ can be formed. It underlies the computation of the total
order of $\bar\jmath - j_0$ and the description of the action of Hecke divisors and of normalised
embeddings in the arithmetic study of these function fields.

